MG={"brick-calculator":"""<h2>How to estimate bricks</h2><p>Each brick plus its share of mortar covers (brick length + joint) × (brick height + joint) of wall face. Work in metres, divide 1 m² by that area to get bricks per m², multiply by the net wall area in m², and add waste for cuts and breakage.</p><p><b>Example:</b> a Vietnamese standard brick is 220 × 105 × 60 mm, laid with a 10 mm joint: 0.23 m × 0.07 m = 0.0161 m², so 1 ÷ 0.0161 = 62.1 bricks per m² for a single-skin wall. A 10 m² wall needs 621 bricks; with 10% waste, 684 bricks.</p><h2>Common brick sizes (single skin, 10 mm joints)</h2><ul><li>Vietnam standard: 220 × 105 × 60 mm — about 62 per m²</li><li>Vietnam 4-hole tuynel: 180 × 80 × 80 mm — about 58 per m²</li><li>UK/EU standard: 215 × 102.5 × 65 mm — about 59 per m²</li><li>US modular: 194 × 57 mm face — about 73 per m²</li><li>US King: 244 × 67 mm face — about 51 per m²</li></ul><p>Sizes vary by manufacturer and region, so enter the dimensions from your supplier's spec sheet. Double-skin (two layers) walls need twice the bricks.</p><h2>Mortar bags</h2><p>One 50 kg bag of mortar mix lays roughly 50 standard bricks. Larger bricks, thicker joints and inexperienced bricklaying all use more, so buy a bag or two extra.</p><h2>Waste and ordering</h2><ul><li>5% for plain walls with few cuts</li><li>10–15% for walls with many openings, corners or patterns</li></ul><p>Order all bricks for one wall from the same production run so the color matches. For brick or concrete pavers on the ground, use the <a href='/paver-calculator/'>paver calculator</a>; for block walls use the <a href='/concrete-block-calculator/'>concrete block calculator</a>.</p>""",
"concrete-calculator":"""<h2>How to calculate concrete for a slab</h2><p>Concrete is ordered by volume. Measure length and width in metres and thickness in centimetres, then convert the thickness to metres (divide by 100). Area (m²) × thickness (m) = volume in m³, the unit ready-mix suppliers use.</p><p><b>Example:</b> a 3 × 3 m patio, 10 cm thick: 3 × 3 = 9 m² × 0.10 m = 0.90 m³. Adding 10% waste gives 0.99 m³.</p><h2>Bags vs. ready-mix truck</h2><p>Typical pre-mixed bag yields: 50 kg ≈ 0.0234 m³, 40 kg ≈ 0.0187 m³, 25 kg ≈ 0.0117 m³ (check your bag label—yields vary slightly by brand). For the example above, 0.99 m³ ÷ 0.0234 m³ = 42.3, rounded up to 43 bags of 50 kg. For jobs over about 0.75 m³ (about 33 bags of 50 kg), a ready-mix delivery is usually cheaper and gives a more uniform pour.</p><h2>Recommended thickness</h2><ul><li>Walkways and patios: 10 cm</li><li>Driveways for passenger cars: 10–13 cm; heavier vehicles: 15 cm</li><li>Shed bases: 10 cm, thickened at the edges</li></ul><h2>Why add waste?</h2><p>Uneven subgrade, form flex and spillage commonly consume 5–10% extra. Running short mid-pour creates a cold joint, so it is cheaper to order a little more.</p>"""}
